WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:24.340
Welcome to a special
session of the Tata Seminar.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.240
Our class is officially
called Technology Design

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.350
and Entrepreneurship Operating
in Emerging Communities.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.210
My name is [? Chintan ?]
[? Veshna, ?] and I co-teach

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.350
this course with Dr. Rob
Starr, Dr. Rich Roth,

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:44.560
who should be here somewhere,
and Professor Charlie Fine.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.450
And there are going to be
about 40 graduate Fellows

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.860
of our program in
the audience soon.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.880
So on behalf of everybody,
it's a great pleasure

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.599
to welcome artist
Olafur Eliasson,

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:09.310
and engineer Frederik Ottesan
to present the seminar.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:09.850
Thank you.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:10.350
Thank you.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.020
So first of all, I want to thank
you for choosing to meet us,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:27.890
given your extremely tight
and short schedule at MIT.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.340
Let me begin by saying a little
bit about the Tata Center

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.230
for Technology and Design.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:36.100
We are focused on creating
technology and systems based

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:41.320
solutions for meeting
the human needs,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.910
particularly in resource
constrained environments.

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:49.780
We started in the fall of
2012, so we are very young,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.000
but we are not small.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.590
We are about 47 graduate
students, about 30 faculty

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.660
from 20 different
departments of MIT,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:07.490
and we're trying to
figure this out together.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:11.560
So the research
of the Tata Center

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:18.940
falls into about seven areas;
that's energy, health, water,

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.510
agriculture, housing
infrastructure,

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:28.030
environmental sustainability,
and entrepreneurship.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.560
Wow that's a lot.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.960
That's a lot, yeah.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.230
And what is particularly
exciting about your visit to

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:44.310
us is that we are imagining
that your journey that you

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.090
are taking as a part of
this Little Sun Project

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.440
is making you contend
with the challenges

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.770
that we are confronting
as a program.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.470
Particularly, we are
focused on understanding

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:02.850
how to identify needs of a
resource constrained community.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.460
How do you work on
solutions in it?

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.610
And then most importantly,
how do you disseminate it?

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.450
So you can imagine that this
crowd has a number of questions

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:21.900
for you, and I propose that we
run this session by giving you

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.900
a chance first to say a
little bit about your work,

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:31.510
and introduce yourself, and then
go right into those questions.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:36.150
So to get us
started, let me maybe

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:40.080
pose a question that will
lead to your introduction.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.470
Could each of you
tell us what you

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:50.790
would want us to know
about yourself as a person?

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:57.300
How would you describe this
thing called the Little Sun?

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.940
I refrain to call it a
product, or a solution,

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.940
or an experience,
or what have you.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:10.760
And how do you see it reflecting
who you are as a person?

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.860
That's already happened
one lecture right there.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:15.720
Go for it.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.130
No, I'm so excited to be here.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.209
Thank you so much.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:21.380
It's great.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.840
I can very quickly say--

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.860
I don't know if I can say
who I would want to be,

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.100
but by all modesty I
think I work as an artist,

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.910
and what I do is art.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:36.710
So there was that part.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:42.530
And then I mean, I grew up in
Denmark with Icelandic parents.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:45.560
I went to art school
after finishing gymnasium.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.880
And I started art school in
89, two weeks before the wall

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:51.260
came down in Berlin.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.500
My first improvised
trip in my education

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.350
was renting a car
driving to Berlin, which

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.700
is approximately a seven
hour drive from Copenhagen.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.430
And I've been in Berlin
since 20 years now.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.710
I went back, finished,
studied a bit,

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.750
but Berlin became
very important for me.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:18.910
So being brought up in Denmark,
having a close relationship

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:22.040
with Iceland, where I came from,
spending 20 years in Germany,

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.610
sort of toss that together,
and sort of shake it a bit,

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:29.500
you come up with my kind of
psychographic mental map.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.790
So there was that part.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:36.040
And then I brought a few
pictures of a few artworks,

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:40.090
and that maybe allows me to
say a little bit about how

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:43.030
I arrived to the project.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.360
So I'll do that in a second.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.420
And did you ask me to say?

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.610
How does Little
Sun reflect on you?

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:55.720
How does Little Sun--

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.275
yeah, I hope that
it does reflect--

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.980
I hope that Little
Sun reflects something

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.290
of an evolution, something
that is changing,

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:08.790
ongoing, and growing.

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:12.000
And in that sense, it's not
about creating something

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.730
static, it's about having
confidence in something

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.320
which is more dynamic.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.122
And this of course,
immediately puts you

00:06:19.122 --> 00:06:20.580
in a situation
where you constantly

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:26.810
have to think of how does
the past produce the future.

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:31.620
How does our memory somehow
create our expectations?

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:38.950
And for me, it's very much
about combining what I know

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.170
with what I would like to do.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.500
And as we all know, it's
one of the great challenges

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:45.100
that we have.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:50.020
And I certainly have this
that we kind of know so much,

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:53.200
and this is now an institution
of knowledge production

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:54.890
to a great extent.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:56.680
But the challenge,
as we also know

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.180
is, how does one then link
what we know to what we do?

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:05.410
And that might sound very
easy, but for me, Little Sun

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.770
very much became a sort of
pivotal point in my life

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:14.530
where what I know actually
channels into a kind of doing

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.655
that reflects the knowing.

00:07:17.655 --> 00:07:19.030
I, of course,
would like to think

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:22.090
of my artistic work, or
my work as an artist,

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:27.220
as a good example of knowing and
doing, or thinking and acting.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:30.550
But Little Sun,
by all means, has

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:36.940
I think proven to me
that, with regards

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:38.710
to identification
as you asked, that

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:42.010
is a very important part of it.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.062
Were you going to show some--?

00:07:44.062 --> 00:07:45.520
Yeah, why don't
you-- maybe Fredrik

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.062
you will introduce
yourself, and I'll

00:07:47.062 --> 00:07:49.360
toss in a few pictures
of a few old artworks,

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.450
and then we can
take it from there.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.100
We'll come back to this
issue of knowing and doing.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.950
Thank you.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:02.710
I'm an engineer, and what
I think is really important

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.290
is natural curiosity.

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:09.770
So just open your eyes to
the world, and what goes on.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:14.780
And I think one of the
places where Olafur and I had

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:20.500
common interest was the doing,
and follow your passions.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:24.970
So right after I
graduated, I felt the need

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.702
to feel at home on
this little globe.

00:08:28.702 --> 00:08:30.160
And I think the
best way of feeling

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.059
at home was to circumnavigate
it in a small boat

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:36.640
because it takes
time, and you get time

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.320
to see and to experience,
and developing whatever

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.780
you want to do in the world.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:50.640
It's quite nice to feel at home,
and feel safe anywhere you go.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.280
Then I think it's
strictly important

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.990
that when you have the
capabilities of doing something

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.780
that you feel is important
that to some extent,

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:04.140
you also have an obligation
to actually do it.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.910
And I didn't make
a solar power lamp

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:12.090
or drag Olafur into making a
solar powered lamp because we

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:17.280
got an idea; the idea
kind of came to us based

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.990
on the knowledge that we had,
and listening to the market,

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:25.890
and listening to the demand,
and seeing the very very

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.930
limited supply.

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:31.680
I thought it was an
important thing to challenge.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.790
And I also think that
you, in that process,

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.270
constantly have to
be curious because we

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.220
are in an environment
where we actually

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:42.890
don't know the results.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.180
You also need to
be slightly daring,

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:53.400
and constantly question
what do you actually do.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.230
Is it right, or is it not right?

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.750
I don't know, but if we
analyze, and if we're

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.900
honest to what we do, and
listen to the environment,

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:05.550
we can come forward to
a reasonable result.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:08.760
And I think that's a
lot of what I represent

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.670
in this is using my technical
expertise in balancing that

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.880
to what can we achieve,
and what can we afford,

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:21.280
and how do we actually do that?

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.120
And so that was
some of the things

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.160
that I found hugely interesting
with venturing into the Little

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.570
Sun Project.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:35.280
At first, it was not my
profession; it was a project.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.600
And we thought it was just a
really interesting project.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.980
It actually came out of another
project that I was doing.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:50.110
I kind of tend to have a passion
for the slightly impossible.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:53.190
And so I was working on
a solar powered airplane,

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:55.770
and we actually just finished
the world's first passenger

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.480
carrying solar powered airplane.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.880
And a lot of the
discussions Olafur and I

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.850
had in the beginning
revolved around the airplane,

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.280
until we realized that
maybe the world needed

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:08.490
a lamp slightly more than an
airplane, at least right now.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:13.360
And so that was how we
got started on working.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:16.930
So I think that to
take the last question,

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:20.160
so how does Little Sun
actually represent that?

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.480
I think without that
natural curiosity,

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:28.590
without that understanding
of the need of talking

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:32.100
to your customers,
without a broad outlook,

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:33.970
it would never have been around.

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:37.510
So it very much embodies
who I feel I am.

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:41.790
And when I realized that
Little Sun embodied who I am,

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.430
I was quite surprised to find
that I was a yellow flower.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.780
I saw myself a lot more
like an iPhone, but--

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:55.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:58.320
Working as an
artist nowadays is,

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:05.040
of course, a kind of training
in multiple sort of areas,

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:10.110
such as not only do you
have to make a work of art,

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:14.040
you also have to sort of
facilitate, or make sure

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:17.700
that the interests that the
work of art has in the world

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.550
is not mismanaged by the museum.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.200
So you kind of,
as an artist, also

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.600
get involved with
running museums,

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.910
making sure that they don't
write a press release that

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.340
is counterproductive to what
you thought the ad was about.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:32.940
And frankly speaking,
having worked then

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:37.030
for 20 years as an artist, you
develop a broad set of skills.

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:39.150
Some of which are very
pragmatic in the sense

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:40.988
of making an exhibition
in Venice, where

00:12:40.988 --> 00:12:42.780
getting a hammer and
a nail is more or less

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.210
impossible in the 11th hour.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:50.430
And I always say, if you can
make an exhibition in Venice,

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.320
you can open up a retail
store in Addis Ababa.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:58.800
Much easier to work in
off grid rural countries

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.798
than it is to be an artist
making an exhibition in Paris.

00:13:03.798 --> 00:13:05.090
And I'm actually quite serious.

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:08.350
So this is one aspect
of it, but of course,

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:11.550
within the heart of
the artworks itself,

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:15.330
there is also the
confidence that one

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:18.240
can have a dialogue
with the world, which

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.250
has consequences to the world.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.620
And this idea of causality,
meaning that when

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:26.880
I say something, and maybe
it's not a major element,

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:30.240
or it's not going to
change the world a lot,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:33.400
but yet you feel that you are
interconnected with the world.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:38.880
And this idea of creativity, or
the way that creativity somehow

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:41.940
performs is really very
much related to the idea

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.720
that reality is relative.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.270
So this of course, leads us to
this idea that a work of art

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:51.390
is not the kind of object
standing in the room.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.640
To sort of break
it down to basics,

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:59.930
which is where the artistic
potential is in the object,

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:02.280
the artistic potential
or the creative potential

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:05.223
is in the way that the object
and the context, this being

00:14:05.223 --> 00:14:07.140
the world or the time
in which it's happening,

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:09.930
all the space in which it takes
place, and so on, and so forth

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:14.100
is exchanging or
resonating you could say.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:17.880
So this means that it is
the way that the work of art

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:22.110
is performing in the world
that makes it to work of art.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:27.450
The work of art itself as it
of itself is only a machine.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:30.120
Without anybody looking
at it, engaging in it,

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:31.650
or a time in which it's--

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:33.840
a world around it--

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:37.230
it's not really, at least for
me, an interesting work of art.

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:39.840
So I work a lot with
spatial principles systems

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:40.950
in which people engage.

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.590
I have studied phenomenology
and psychology with regards

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:45.230
to how do we experience.

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:47.897
The kind of behavioral studies--
what does experiencing actually

00:14:47.897 --> 00:14:48.397
mean?

00:14:48.397 --> 00:14:49.837
What is the experience economy?

00:14:49.837 --> 00:14:50.920
What is counterproductive?

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:52.210
What is productive in this?

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:54.370
So there's a lot of
social scientific material

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:57.340
in this, which taps very
well into communication.

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:58.897
How do you speak
without patronizing

00:14:58.897 --> 00:14:59.980
the one you're talking to?

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:01.630
What is non-violent
communication?

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.900
How do you exercise a degree
of empathic understanding

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:05.500
of the other?

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:07.000
And what is the
notion of the other?

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:07.790
What is identity?

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:08.790
And so on, and so forth.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.720
So as you know, art,
of course today,

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:15.490
is a kind of incredibly
rich and very resourceful

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:17.260
field, where there
is a lot of studies.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:19.870
And when we say
something about art,

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:25.170
it is clear that it's a little
bit like saying the word food.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.890
So there's so many Kitchens.

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:31.120
There's so many different types
of looking at food, and so on.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.370
So art is really not--

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.050
art is just language.

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:38.270
And I have come to
appreciate this idea.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:40.660
It's not really so much about
what language you speak,

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:43.450
it's more about what do you
want to say with the language.

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:44.230
So I'm trying to--

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:46.390
I'm so sorry-- I'm trying
to break it very short.

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:47.470
So this is a project.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:48.837
I have four examples for you.

00:15:48.837 --> 00:15:50.920
This is a project-- this
is actually 10 years old,

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:53.620
a little bit of an anniversary
at the Tate Modern Turbine Hall

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:54.620
in London.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:58.840
So what it is, as you can see,
sort of a sunlike, actually

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:01.390
yellow piece of plastic backlit
with a little bit of haze

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:05.230
in the room, half a circle,
the lower half of a circle,

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:07.510
and then I put a
very large mirror

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:09.933
in the ceiling, which gives
you the sort of impression

00:16:09.933 --> 00:16:11.100
that there is a full circle.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.260
But if you look closely, or
if you just look actually

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:17.630
carefully, you can see the
other half is not really there.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:19.520
It's just a reflection
of the lower half.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:24.310
But interestingly, what happened
is that people very quickly

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:26.650
started to engage in this
space in various ways,

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.130
and it became a
kind of a community

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.260
on the floor that started
behaving differently

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:35.230
than what one normally does
in museums, and so on, and so

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:36.160
forth.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:38.260
So and then, of
course, I could talk

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:39.470
about this in different ways.

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:41.320
But what I think
was particularly

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:46.110
interesting is that there was
no doubt that people were having

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:49.610
an experience with, as
far as I could tell,

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:51.740
was relatively robust.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:54.050
Some people didn't maybe
like it as much as others,

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:55.550
but generally
speaking, it was clear

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:57.650
that there was an experience.

00:16:57.650 --> 00:16:59.330
There's a lot of
things going on.

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:01.170
People started to
behave differently.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.380
Some got more noisy;
some got more quiet.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:06.109
But what constituted
the space was

00:17:06.109 --> 00:17:08.240
this sense of sharing
an experience,

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:12.329
without necessarily
agreeing on what we saw.

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:14.140
So this is an
interesting phenomenon

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:16.940
where I think you can have
a high degree of singularity

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:19.119
in the subjective
experience, but there

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:22.483
was a high degree of plurality
in the sense of sharing.

00:17:22.483 --> 00:17:23.900
And as you know,
this is something

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:25.910
that has become
relatively rare in society

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:27.619
because everything
is either polarized,

00:17:27.619 --> 00:17:30.380
or it's becoming probabilistic,
or nationalistic, or whatever.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:34.610
Meaning that things very quickly
become exclusive or excluding.

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:36.828
Meaning that if you don't
agree, you're not welcome.

00:17:36.828 --> 00:17:39.120
If you're not the same as
me, or if you look different,

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:40.495
if you have
different background,

00:17:40.495 --> 00:17:42.950
and so on, and so forth,
there's a tendency to exclusion.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:46.250
So as a space, or as an
art institutional system,

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:49.070
it's very interesting that
some institutional systems can

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:51.590
kind of facilitate a
parliamentary, good, democratic

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:54.470
idea about, well, let's see
if we can share something,

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.690
without necessarily
having to agree with it.

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:59.070
Let's share and disagree
at the same time.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.340
So this is a discussion
one could go down into.

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:02.870
I think a very
interesting principle

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:06.045
that I've been very
curious to look into,

00:18:06.045 --> 00:18:07.670
which is why I've
done works like this.

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:08.420
You can barely see it.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:10.100
Maybe it's a little
bit of a rainbow,

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:12.410
or it's a small
rainbow, which is

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:15.350
made with water falling,
spotlight, a little bit

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:18.000
like a sort of normal
spotlight in the ceiling.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:21.170
And as we know, you
need an eye as well

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:23.060
because if there is
nobody in this space,

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:25.610
the question is whether the
rainbow is actually there.

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:29.060
Now, that's a good discussion
between the natural obsessive

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:32.060
natural sciences, or the
kind of phenomenologist

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:36.290
as to what extent is the eye
actually a coproducer here?

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:39.320
But principally speaking,
the object is gone.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:41.960
It's been dematerialised.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:46.820
The traditional notion of
what constitutes art is gone.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:48.620
The subject becomes more active.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:51.890
My seeing is becoming
the producer.

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:53.278
I become the artist.

00:18:53.278 --> 00:18:54.945
The spectator has
become the coproducer.

00:18:54.945 --> 00:18:59.540
I as an artist, me, hand
over the responsibility

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:02.720
of the quality of this
project to the people who

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:04.460
come to visit the exhibition.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:10.170
Meaning that I, as an artist,
I'm only, you know of course,

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:12.680
I kind of write a
narrative, but the reader

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:15.870
is the kind of performer
of the narrative.

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:18.830
And if there is a person next to
me, as we know with a rainbow,

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:20.930
the rainbow that the
person next to me sees

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:23.810
is, by definition, not the same
because the angle of the eye,

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:25.750
and the drop is a different one.

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:28.010
So same phenomena,
but different rainbow.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:29.840
And we can still
see something where

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:31.430
we constitute the
idea of sameness,

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:37.050
but it is by virtue, as far as
we know, not at all the same.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:38.050
And so on, and so forth.

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:41.990
So this is the waterfall
in New York five years ago,

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:44.990
I believe 2008, so
six years slowly.

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:47.600
And one could talk
about this sort

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:51.920
of spatial phenomenological
principles,

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:54.080
but what I think is
interesting here,

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:55.970
or one of the things
that was interesting,

00:19:55.970 --> 00:20:00.230
to pick one topic again, was
when we see water falling,

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:03.020
we actually also see
how far away it is.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:05.030
Because, as we know,
water tends to fall

00:20:05.030 --> 00:20:07.770
with roughly the same rate--
a little more water does

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:09.770
apparently fall a little
faster-- but, you know,

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.410
when you're in a landscape, you
travel to Iceland, no trees,

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:13.640
no cars, no roads.

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:16.850
And you are looking, and you go,
my god, what landscape is this?

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:17.840
Am I on the moon?

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.470
I can't really get
a sense of scale.

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:21.470
I wonder if I go down
there, will I be this big,

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:22.430
or will I be that big?

00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:26.180
Does it take an hour, or a
day to walk into that valley?

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:28.490
Haven't you been at landscapes
where you just-- it's

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:32.690
somehow if you see a tree in
the area, and if you have a car,

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:35.630
you can kind of tell,
then you look, and then

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:36.620
you see a waterfall.

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:38.210
And you look at the
waterfall, and you

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.040
realize the water's
falling very slowly.

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:43.520
Oh it must be a very tall
waterfall, very far away.

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:46.100
Suddenly, the waterfall
introduces a sense of scale,

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:48.560
and obviously it's not the
scale of the landscape that

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:51.912
is interesting, it's the scale
of your body within the context

00:20:51.912 --> 00:20:52.620
of the landscape.

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.800
So it constitutes
your own presence,

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:57.110
which is why hiking
through a landscape,

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:58.640
and suddenly looking
at a waterfall

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:00.920
can be such a
rewarding experience,

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:01.920
and so on, and so forth.

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:05.048
So depth, perception,
space, and body presence

00:21:05.048 --> 00:21:07.340
is one of the things that
I've been very interested in.

00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:09.673
And if you look at New York,
where public space has been

00:21:09.673 --> 00:21:13.220
privatized, and kind of the
regime of two dimensionality,

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:16.280
depriving social,
let's say, confidence,

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:18.750
which is a big discussion
within public parks and space,

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:19.250
and so on.

00:21:19.250 --> 00:21:21.350
It's an interesting
field, and I think

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:23.390
art, of course, is
one of the factors

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:26.960
that can introduce a sense
of, not just [? deafness, ?]

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:30.800
but also a sense of a space
where people can actually meet,

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:34.550
singular, plural, maybe even.

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:39.000
Same principle, rainbow
around circular sculpture,

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:41.390
architecture thing.

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:44.990
You walk around-- if you don't
walk, the color won't change,

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:45.830
as you can see.

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:47.790
Just stand still,
nothing happens.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:49.910
So if you're not
walking, you're not

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:53.520
activating, so you
again the artist.

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.850
I worked for many years
with a number of scientists.

00:21:56.850 --> 00:22:00.030
I've always, like
a vampire, sucked

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.850
anything I could out of natural
scientists, cosmologists,

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:04.060
physicists, and so on.

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:08.250
I've tried to take advantage
of the methodological successes

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:09.900
that they have
brought to the table.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:12.480
I, as an artist, aren't
really capable of much,

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:17.460
but believing in that creativity
plays a significant role in how

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:19.410
the world is being shaped.

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:21.480
But the rest of the
stuff I rely on,

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:25.560
incredible other talents
I have, as I grow older,

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:29.520
I have come also to
appreciate them, and respect

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:32.010
the role of, for instance,
the engineer in Frederik's

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:35.930
case, the fact that the
kind of resonating effort

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:39.160
is something that is
very, very valuable.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:41.220
And when I talk
about effort, I think

00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:43.080
I say most specifically
because it's

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:45.630
not about the objective
knowledge that we have.

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:47.820
It's not the sum of
everything we know.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.460
It is the effort that
knowing something gives us.

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:54.250
The effort to actually
push something ahead.

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:58.140
So I have come to
understand, through my art,

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:01.470
that sometimes it's not
understanding a work of art,

00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:04.740
or it's not the art work,
but it is the effort

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:08.490
to understand the work of art
that makes me appreciate it.

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:11.370
This is why the effort
of actually trying

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:14.640
to bring an idea into
doing is sometimes

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:17.880
more valuable than the
done idea, or the idea when

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:19.080
it is done.

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:24.360
I think this is a very
interesting argument

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:27.010
because very often we have
people who have great ideas.

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:29.190
My students at the University
in Berlin, they say,

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:30.750
I have made an
amazing work of art.

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:32.220
Let me just explain it to you.

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:33.270
It's an idea.

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:34.140
It goes like this.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:35.623
Isn't that a great work of art?

00:23:35.623 --> 00:23:36.540
And I say, no, no, no.

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:39.810
It's an idea about a potentially
interesting work of art,

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:42.660
but why don't you try to
put some effort into it,

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.890
and see if it
actually translates

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:46.560
into something interesting?

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:49.323
Because once you
start doing that,

00:23:49.323 --> 00:23:50.740
you will see two
things happening.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:52.440
First of all, it changes.

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:55.560
Secondly, the effort is not
as easy as one should think.

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:58.050
The effort is actually
really hard work, complex.

00:23:58.050 --> 00:23:59.550
You have to have
engineers on board.

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:01.592
And then they motivate
you, but they also kind of

00:24:01.592 --> 00:24:03.810
make it very difficult
because they also

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:06.990
bring in sort of data that is
just not what you had expected.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:09.052
I think you do that too.

00:24:09.052 --> 00:24:10.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:13.590
So I was thinking
about what's in common

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.460
about art and engineering?

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:18.480
And one of the things that
interestingly occurred to me

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:23.020
was that this whole
idea of ideas to action,

00:24:23.020 --> 00:24:28.230
which is the title of this
session, or Menz et Manos,

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:33.540
which is the model of
MIT, mind and hands,

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:36.580
is where these two
disciplines may come together.

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:38.650
So here's my question.

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:44.250
What, to you, comes in
between an idea and action,

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:48.750
and is this where art and
engineering, or disciplines

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:49.500
come together?

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:51.510
Is this where they
actually differ

00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:53.160
so that something
interesting can

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:55.320
come from their interaction?

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:57.180
How do you think about that?

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:58.493
I think a lot about that.

00:24:58.493 --> 00:24:59.910
And I think one
of the things that

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:06.650
is in between having an idea,
and actually doing something

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:08.390
actually, to a
lesser extent, has

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:10.410
to do with the
choice of material,

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:13.550
then it has to do with the
degree of self-confidence.

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:21.800
So surprisingly, the kind of
trust you have in yourself,

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:24.080
with regards to
stepping from the idea

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:31.060
into giving it language, is
actually a much different--

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:35.670
it's quite adventurous to
have an idea, then take a pen,

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:37.410
and then make a drawing.

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:40.020
It's like an unbelievably
rich moment when you

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:42.660
start to give the idea shape.

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:44.340
One can even go
backwards a little bit.

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:46.410
So where did the idea come from?

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:49.110
And sometimes, if you don't
know exactly what to draw--

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:52.440
in my case, it can
sort of plays off you

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:54.330
going back, wow,
this idea really

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:56.370
came out of that feeling.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:59.730
Before it was language because
I can speak about my idea.

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:02.700
It actually was a sort
of non-verbal sort

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:05.100
of state of something.

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:08.220
And this was, to some
extent, intuitive.

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:10.590
I don't know if I could
barely even think about it,

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:11.850
but I had the feeling.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:13.920
And this is a lovely state.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:18.820
I'm sorry to kind of get
more and more vague here,

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:21.430
but let's just wander down
this path a little bit.

00:26:21.430 --> 00:26:25.500
So sometimes we
also must appreciate

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:30.370
we have a feeling, which has
not yet found a language yet.

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:33.720
It's incredible, and
we should nurture it.

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:35.820
So where does this come from?

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:41.270
Maybe it is a kind
of micro trauma.

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:43.880
So 10 years ago, I
was in a situation.

00:26:43.880 --> 00:26:47.180
Something happened, and it
kind of left an imprint,

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:49.910
like a footprint in--

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:53.450
so depending on where, and
how we use our language--

00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:56.120
in my soul, I could say.

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.550
So this footprint--
and then I realized,

00:26:58.550 --> 00:27:01.370
10 years later, I'm going
there, oh interesting.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:05.550
That just stayed with
me, that footprint.

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:07.980
And now, I can add a word to it.

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:11.640
I can feel this.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:15.220
Let me just go back, and
visit that footprint.

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:19.080
What was the circumstances?

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:20.700
What inspired me?

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:24.420
Why was I-- a micro trauma
can be both good and bad,

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:26.130
and it's so very complex.

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.170
So there is something there.

00:27:28.170 --> 00:27:30.510
And we can exercise
this a little bit

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:33.600
as I think one should
not hesitate to do.

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:35.670
So see where do I get
my inspiration from.

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:39.810
We have to seek the
kind of the places

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:43.470
where inspiration is brought
to us, whether it's preverbal

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:44.580
or not.

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:46.390
We can also think about that.

00:27:46.390 --> 00:27:49.810
Let's think of 10
years from now,

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:51.643
where we are sitting
somewhere else,

00:27:51.643 --> 00:27:53.560
and we're going to look
back at this classroom

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:55.090
where we're sitting now.

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:58.810
What do we think
is needed in order

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:02.620
to make this moment
of such a quality

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:07.810
that our footprint is taken
with us into 10 years from now?

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:11.670
From now, I can sort of send
a greeting into the future.

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:13.530
I say hi to myself.

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:17.010
And say, oh this is now,
we have to say something.

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:19.980
And this means that we have
to take the moment serious.

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:23.310
We have to treasure this
incredibly important moment

00:28:23.310 --> 00:28:25.680
because maybe we, right
now, are about to do

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:27.430
something significant.

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:30.630
So I'm just saying
the idea from thinking

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:34.620
to doing is, especially
at its beginning,

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:38.250
incredibly complex
and wonderful.

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:42.330
So from there then, of
course, very quickly, it also

00:28:42.330 --> 00:28:45.990
has to do with a certain
talent with regards

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:49.290
to rationalizing the steps.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:52.650
Should it be a tabletop
experimental process?

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:53.760
Should it be a spatial?

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:55.660
Should it be a big
laboratory at MIT?

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:59.100
Do I need a 100 people team,
or just need one person?

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:04.980
So all these sort of
methodological questions that.

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:08.100
And I think this, of course,
depends, and has a lot

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:11.460
to do with what idea, but
one thing that I think

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:13.443
is very important
is that you are--

00:29:13.443 --> 00:29:16.110
and then I think you should talk
about it for a little bit also.

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:20.990
I think for one, you are
on the way from the idea,

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:22.940
and in my case, a work of art.

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:24.390
But it could really be anything.

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:26.348
So you're on it, but
you're only halfway there,

00:29:26.348 --> 00:29:30.440
and oh my God, this is never
ever going to be a work of art.

00:29:30.440 --> 00:29:32.900
And then you're kind of stuck.

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:34.280
And there's a
certain point where

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:36.170
also talking to a
natural scientist

00:29:36.170 --> 00:29:39.090
just won't help you because
you know it's your choice.

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:42.630
I said, oh you can't ask
somebody to solve it for you.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:46.310
So this is, I think, an
interesting moment also

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:50.690
in the process because
the solution is not

00:29:50.690 --> 00:29:55.370
in-- the solution is not
looking at the problem,

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:58.550
but it is looking at the
world in which the problem is

00:29:58.550 --> 00:29:59.640
located.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:03.590
So sometimes, the making
sense of a problem

00:30:03.590 --> 00:30:08.690
lies in seeing the problem in
the context of the world, which

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:13.400
brings you from asking
how to asking why.

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:16.070
So generally speaking,
we go from thinking

00:30:16.070 --> 00:30:17.185
to doing as asking how.

00:30:17.185 --> 00:30:18.060
It's going to be red.

00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:18.860
It's going to be wood.

00:30:18.860 --> 00:30:20.068
I'm going to make it so tall.

00:30:20.068 --> 00:30:21.613
And so there's all this house.

00:30:21.613 --> 00:30:23.780
And then at some point, you
just run short of-- it's

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:28.260
just like kind of you've driven
yourself into the desert.

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:30.720
Then you start
wondering, well, why?

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:32.400
And then you have
a new momentum.

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:33.710
Oh now I know why.

00:30:33.710 --> 00:30:35.670
Of course, then well,
maybe I shouldn't have

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:36.962
made it red in the first place.

00:30:36.962 --> 00:30:39.030
Maybe it should be yellow.

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:47.040
So these are, in a very abstract
sense, I think the kind of,

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:50.670
and as you can tell, the
kind of methodology where

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:53.340
the trust plays a major role.

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:56.340
Do I have confidence
in making a choice?

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:58.230
And do I have confidence,
and even doing

00:30:58.230 --> 00:30:59.790
it looking at the world?

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:03.450
And see how the world feeds
you to make the choice,

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:05.860
and how the choice
also impacts the world.

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:07.200
I call that touching.

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:09.300
You touch the world.

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:12.180
You hold hands with
the sun, or you

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.640
are being touched by the world.

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:15.840
You are interconnected.

00:31:20.915 --> 00:31:22.540
I think one of the
things you mentioned

00:31:22.540 --> 00:31:24.790
that, in this
practice as an artist,

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:31.900
you were using engineers, and
physicists, and mathematicians.

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:34.510
As I'm not an artist,
I'm an engineer,

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:36.670
I could also see
that, as an engineer,

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:40.990
I think it could be really
beneficial to use an artist.

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:46.060
And that's kind of
what I came about.

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:52.810
Because I started this
solar powered lamp idea,

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:57.137
and it came to my mind that
I wanted to make an impact.

00:31:57.137 --> 00:31:59.470
And I looked in the marketplace,
and I didn't-- it's not

00:31:59.470 --> 00:32:01.930
an invention in any way.

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:04.360
Solar powered lamps has
been around for a while.

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:07.660
And how could we then
make a real impact?

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:14.140
And what were the drivers in
buying products like this,

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:16.470
or artworks like this?

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:19.290
And I realized looking
into the marketplace,

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:22.110
there was absolutely no
product in the market that

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:25.440
inspired any kind of happiness,
or any kind of beauty,

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:28.170
or any kind of emotions.

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:31.270
Human beings are
hugely emotional.

00:32:31.270 --> 00:32:33.240
And then I kind of looked
back at my history,

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:36.850
and my education, and learning.

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:39.220
It there's just a blank page.

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:44.710
I never learned anything about
designing $0.35 of plastic

00:32:44.710 --> 00:32:46.610
into giving any
kind of emotions.

00:32:46.610 --> 00:32:47.710
I just couldn't do it.

00:32:47.710 --> 00:32:49.370
I could sit and draw forever.

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:51.520
I could look at my study books.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:54.370
I had not learned
how do I put emotions

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.380
into a piece of plastic.

00:32:56.380 --> 00:32:58.573
So I'm going, I can't do this.

00:32:58.573 --> 00:33:00.490
I could make squares,
and I could make rounds.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:02.830
I could calculate the volumes
in squares and rounds,

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:05.890
and stuff like that, but it
just didn't change the picture.

00:33:05.890 --> 00:33:08.410
So that's where kind
of, of course, I

00:33:08.410 --> 00:33:11.710
look at Olafur because
very, very few people are

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:17.980
capable of putting a huge
amount of emotions into a mold,

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:23.170
and reproduce their emotions
a million times at no cost.

00:33:23.170 --> 00:33:25.210
So I think, as
engineers, there's

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:29.980
a huge opportunity in looking
into production of emotions,

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:34.230
and that's where the artwork
comes into the picture.

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:38.120
So that a little bit underlines
the collaboration of how

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:39.800
we work, and the necessity.

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:42.410
When you were talking about
your other art projects,

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:45.500
you talked a lot about
the idea of trying

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:48.680
to make part of the art the
experience of the people that

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:50.810
observe art.

00:33:50.810 --> 00:33:54.890
So as you're thinking about the
design process for Little Sun,

00:33:54.890 --> 00:33:58.430
who is it that is
experiencing this art,

00:33:58.430 --> 00:34:02.210
and what was the experience
that you wanted them to have?

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:03.980
We started working
with the Little Sun,

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:08.790
and we were spending some
time in Ethiopia testing it.

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:13.310
And so, for instance, we
went to see a German version

00:34:13.310 --> 00:34:14.389
of what is called USAID.

00:34:14.389 --> 00:34:16.940
You know, kind of
engineers working locally,

00:34:16.940 --> 00:34:20.000
and really talented
people, and so on, it's

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:21.230
called [? GT7 ?] in Germany.

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:23.840
And we were sitting there,
and they said, oh just

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:26.270
make sure it just bloody works.

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:27.320
Who cares about the sun?

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:29.000
This is Africa.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:32.960
So it's typically a sort of a
kind of neocolonial attitude,

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:34.219
condescending and so on.

00:34:34.219 --> 00:34:35.719
We kind of couldn't see it even.

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:38.630
We were like, oh yeah,
yeah, yeah, kind of.

00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:39.870
This was a few years ago now.

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:44.400
And then Frederik went out,
and drove to south of Ethiopia.

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:47.690
And then he came back in the
evening, or the next day,

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:49.370
and he said, you
know, what's so funny?

00:34:49.370 --> 00:34:51.912
We had something which looked
a little bit like an ice hockey

00:34:51.912 --> 00:34:52.580
puck.

00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:54.590
It's like really
sort of practical.

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:56.340
You could toss it
around, nothing happens,

00:34:56.340 --> 00:35:00.860
so totally unemotional.

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:04.010
And then he said,
Frederik, well you

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:07.592
know, I was showing some young
girls, sort of on the way

00:35:07.592 --> 00:35:09.050
home from school
I guess, the lamp,

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:11.620
and they said, oh
well, interesting lamp,

00:35:11.620 --> 00:35:14.040
but the string is really nice.

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:16.760
And then Frederik took out his
iPhone, and he wrote that down,

00:35:16.760 --> 00:35:19.530
and then they said, the
iPhone is really amazing.

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:22.040
And I was like, oh, we're
doing something wrong.

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:25.380
And obviously, it just
dawned on me very quickly.

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:29.720
My god, it was so typical to
think that functionality alone

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:30.500
will do.

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:33.200
So typically, engineers to
think that they can somehow

00:35:33.200 --> 00:35:35.600
tell, or speak on
behalf of other people.

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:37.640
No offense, but you
know what I mean.

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:40.190
So the kind of
first learning curve

00:35:40.190 --> 00:35:42.740
with regards to the design
was as Frederik actually

00:35:42.740 --> 00:35:43.460
pointed out.

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:46.010
Well, of course,
the success lays

00:35:46.010 --> 00:35:50.330
in the ability to touch each
other, or to be touched.

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:54.040
And this is interesting
because this everybody shares.

00:35:54.040 --> 00:35:57.470
It's not about the kind
of resourceful North,

00:35:57.470 --> 00:35:59.840
and the resourceless South.

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:02.570
It just becomes about the
desires we have in life.

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:04.120
So I want to be happy.

00:36:04.120 --> 00:36:04.620
That's it.

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:07.590
I want to have beautiful
stuff around me, relatively

00:36:07.590 --> 00:36:08.090
beautiful.

00:36:08.090 --> 00:36:10.430
I just like-- I just want
a certain amount of luxury

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:13.700
in my life because it just makes
me happier, simple as that.

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:18.710
So the design very quickly
became about also saying, well,

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:23.060
this project is about the
same needs everywhere.

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:27.590
It's not like only a problem
when you're in off-grid x-area,

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:29.580
where there's no
access to energy.

00:36:29.580 --> 00:36:32.060
It's kind of a global problem
that a part of the world

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:35.900
is totally disconnected
from power, disregarding

00:36:35.900 --> 00:36:38.450
the health, the
kind of, you know,

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:43.730
the climate, the educational,
all the sort of surrounding

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:44.730
aspects.

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:48.117
So the driving
process really started

00:36:48.117 --> 00:36:50.450
with saying, well, let's just
make a lamp that everybody

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:52.070
thinks is beautiful.

00:36:52.070 --> 00:36:55.670
I think I'll rephrase that a
little bit because the truth

00:36:55.670 --> 00:36:57.650
is, to answer your
question, we did

00:36:57.650 --> 00:37:02.240
have what you would consider
as a focus group in Hawassa,

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:05.540
a small town South of
Addis Ababa, Ethiopia.

00:37:05.540 --> 00:37:08.810
And we did also look at the
demographics of Ethiopia

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:12.080
because that was
our starting point.

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:15.350
And looking at that, you
realize that the population

00:37:15.350 --> 00:37:18.620
is on average below 16.

00:37:18.620 --> 00:37:22.280
So you need to make a product
that speaks incredibly well

00:37:22.280 --> 00:37:24.050
to children.

00:37:24.050 --> 00:37:29.150
And the people buying products
for the children is mothers.

00:37:29.150 --> 00:37:32.000
So designing
something that would

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:35.240
fall in the taste of
women and children,

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:38.360
then you cover about
80% of your market.

00:37:38.360 --> 00:37:40.580
And with any kind
of product, it's

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:42.168
quite nice to know
that you cover

00:37:42.168 --> 00:37:43.460
a certain amount of the market.

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:48.170
And so the lamp is built
in a direct dialogue

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:51.890
with those women and
children in Hawassa.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:55.400
And as all of us said, we
were going back and forth

00:37:55.400 --> 00:38:00.230
between Berlin and Addis
quite a couple of times,

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:03.530
realizing that the first
prototypes we made just

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:04.400
didn't work.

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:06.770
Not that they didn't
work technically--

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:09.785
and when we were showing
the products in the market,

00:38:09.785 --> 00:38:11.660
and talking about the
products in the market,

00:38:11.660 --> 00:38:14.390
they were very, very
sellable because we

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:17.660
were able to communicate
the upside of the product--

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:19.790
but as soon as we laid
the product by itself,

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:21.920
in an open market, on a
little kiosk or something,

00:38:21.920 --> 00:38:25.740
it wouldn't move because it
didn't communicate by itself.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:26.610
So nothing happened.

00:38:26.610 --> 00:38:28.110
And I think that's
very much in line

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:30.800
with what we see on the
other products in the market.

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:33.730
They don't communicate, and
that means they're not sellable.

00:38:33.730 --> 00:38:37.210
Because our customers,
they don't care about LEDs.

00:38:37.210 --> 00:38:38.290
They can't judge it.

00:38:38.290 --> 00:38:40.213
They don't care about
battery qualities

00:38:40.213 --> 00:38:41.380
because they can't judge it.

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:43.930
They don't care about
solar panel output

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:46.003
because it doesn't
make any sense to them.

00:38:46.003 --> 00:38:47.170
So they care about emotions.

00:38:47.170 --> 00:38:49.300
They need something
that they will pick up

00:38:49.300 --> 00:38:52.390
because they think it's nice,
or beautiful, or interesting,

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:55.180
or emotional because
that's how we are.

00:38:55.180 --> 00:38:58.870
So going back and forth a
couple of times, realizing

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:01.330
that we didn't have
the right product,

00:39:01.330 --> 00:39:05.320
we were looking at
photos, I think--

00:39:05.320 --> 00:39:07.120
who was in charge of this thing?

00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:12.370
I'll just move to a photo
I can quickly browse.

00:39:12.370 --> 00:39:14.500
Yeah, can you take
the photo of me

00:39:14.500 --> 00:39:15.895
in the street with the girls?

00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:24.090
Because one of the things
that is really interesting

00:39:24.090 --> 00:39:27.540
learning is understanding
who your customers are,

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:30.120
and what they stand for.

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:35.230
And our customers,
they're romantic.

00:39:35.230 --> 00:39:39.190
Their patterns, their colors,
the layers, the flowers,

00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:42.430
they're by no means minimalists.

00:39:42.430 --> 00:39:46.640
And that you have to understand,
and that you have to address.

00:39:46.640 --> 00:39:49.330
And so the next
time we came back.

00:39:49.330 --> 00:39:51.590
So we looked at that
picture, and realized

00:39:51.590 --> 00:39:54.730
that that was the case, and
what we were doing wrong.

00:39:54.730 --> 00:40:01.400
And we went Addis asking
completely different things

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:03.410
that we've done at
any other point.

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:06.950
So we'd start asking,
how does prosperity look?

00:40:06.950 --> 00:40:08.720
How does beauty look?

00:40:08.720 --> 00:40:10.280
What is your favorite color?

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:13.700
That kind of questions we
would ask our customer group.

00:40:13.700 --> 00:40:20.570
And as Olfaf was telling
for physical bodies,

00:40:20.570 --> 00:40:24.080
then turn those questions
into something that looked

00:40:24.080 --> 00:40:26.280
somewhat similar to this.

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:27.900
It's also about the story.

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:31.190
So I think you
detailed what I said.

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:34.760
It wasn't really different
from what I said.

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:36.410
That degree of the
detail of the design

00:40:36.410 --> 00:40:40.190
process, and not forgetting
there is a practical side,

00:40:40.190 --> 00:40:43.410
we also wanted to compete
with the oil industry.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:48.830
So there's a bit, sort of,
money, monetary, financial.

00:40:48.830 --> 00:40:50.810
But still, there
was also this story,

00:40:50.810 --> 00:40:54.200
and the design is obviously
the relationship with the lamp,

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:57.650
as much as the kind of
actual tangible feeling of it

00:40:57.650 --> 00:40:58.670
in your hand.

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:02.160
So the story, at a
very early stage,

00:41:02.160 --> 00:41:05.670
that I was with an elderly
woman, and I said to her,

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:08.240
I have to explain it, and
then it's also a work of art.

00:41:08.240 --> 00:41:12.270
And she looked at me, she said,
what do you mean a work of art?

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:16.320
And I said, well,
it's a work of art.

00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:19.380
And this was in Addis Ababa.

00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:22.050
And she looked at me, and
says, so she said to me,

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:23.808
like in the church?

00:41:23.808 --> 00:41:26.100
And then I looked at her,
and I didn't see that coming.

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:29.380
And then I said, yes,
exactly like in the church.

00:41:29.380 --> 00:41:32.230
Then she looked, and said,
oh but that's amazing.

00:41:32.230 --> 00:41:36.480
So you see sometimes this idea
that something nonfunctional

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:38.550
carries you much further.

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:40.070
And obviously, it
still has to work,

00:41:40.070 --> 00:41:43.660
and has to compete with a
certain price benchmark,

00:41:43.660 --> 00:41:44.470
and so on.

00:41:44.470 --> 00:41:51.480
And this is where this idea of
combining this idea of power--

00:41:51.480 --> 00:41:53.910
I have five hours of
sunlight in my hand.

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:56.040
I'm holding hands with the sun.

00:41:56.040 --> 00:41:58.740
When I charge it,
I charge myself.

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:02.340
The idea of resources
being, on one side,

00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:04.140
the kind of measurable
amount of sunlight

00:42:04.140 --> 00:42:06.660
that you have in your
battery, and sort of

00:42:06.660 --> 00:42:08.970
suggest that there is a
relationship between that,

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:10.290
and how you feel.

00:42:10.290 --> 00:42:11.520
You are charging yourself.

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:12.930
You are becoming resourceful.

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:17.970
That became a kind of
design driver with regards

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:20.410
to understanding
what is our message.

00:42:20.410 --> 00:42:23.430
How do we reach out
in a way that is--

00:42:27.840 --> 00:42:30.180
productive sounds
so quantifiable--

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:32.880
but reach out in a
way which actually

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:35.680
is confidence building.

00:42:35.680 --> 00:42:38.610
When you say that
you have a sun--

00:42:38.610 --> 00:42:41.550
you have the power of
the sun in your hand,

00:42:41.550 --> 00:42:43.530
and then when I'm
thinking that, oh but it

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:45.630
has this solar
panel, and batteries,

00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:47.970
and there's a casing,
and there's a LED.

00:42:47.970 --> 00:42:49.410
I mean, I understand it.

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:52.232
We have a really funny
picture of a sign

00:42:52.232 --> 00:42:54.690
because we didn't have marketing
material in the beginning,

00:42:54.690 --> 00:42:55.800
we just had lamps.

00:42:55.800 --> 00:42:59.130
And then we started testing out
selling these lamps at kiosks,

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:01.500
and they created marketing
material themselves.

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:06.330
So a really nice one, a
big picture, and our logo,

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:12.900
and it says, no batteries,
no electricity, only sun.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:17.250
It's just completely wrong,
but that's the perception.

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:19.110
So I think you, as
an engineer, have

00:43:19.110 --> 00:43:21.420
to understand that a
lot of your customers,

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:24.450
at least in this market, have
a very different perception

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:26.160
of technology than you do.

00:43:26.160 --> 00:43:29.640
I did a lot of road trips in
Ethiopia in the beginning,

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:34.680
and I talk to our
focus group, and one

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:38.940
of the questions I came
about quite frequently was,

00:43:38.940 --> 00:43:41.550
is it dangerous to wear it?

00:43:41.550 --> 00:43:44.100
And that surprised
me quite a lot

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:46.360
because I couldn't
understand it.

00:43:46.360 --> 00:43:49.620
And another question I
got that really showed me

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:53.460
our customer group's
understanding of technology

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:57.720
was, will it work
without the strap?

00:43:57.720 --> 00:44:01.650
And to you and me, that seems
like a quite odd question,

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:06.000
but if technology is pretty
distant from what you do,

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:07.660
it's a perfectly valid question.

00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:13.410
And it just shows you how far
away LEDs and batteries are,

00:44:13.410 --> 00:44:15.210
if you can think that
the lamp will not

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:17.255
work without the strap.

00:44:17.255 --> 00:44:19.380
Hello, I'm a graduate
student of the MIT Media Lab,

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:20.370
and I'm also an artist.

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:23.400
My question is based on the
consumer feedback, and the way

00:44:23.400 --> 00:44:26.220
that people are using it, what
is the future of the Little Sun

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:27.280
Project?

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:30.000
You plan to design another one?

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:30.540
Oh yeah.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:33.228
So of course, we have
like so many plans.

00:44:33.228 --> 00:44:35.220
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:39.270
But I think one
very clear ambition

00:44:39.270 --> 00:44:41.790
is to succeed in scale.

00:44:41.790 --> 00:44:43.140
I mean, now we have--

00:44:43.140 --> 00:44:45.660
we feel we're so we're
so excited, and proud,

00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:46.620
and all of that.

00:44:46.620 --> 00:44:49.950
We have kind of managed
to bring about--

00:44:49.950 --> 00:44:52.260
what is it, 130?

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:53.970
250,000 lamps.

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:55.470
We have 250,000 now.

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:59.000
So and that has-- if they are
all out there, and working,

00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:02.280
and so on, we have
already tossed a few--

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:05.640
we have reduced the
sales of petroleum.

00:45:05.640 --> 00:45:09.490
It's not a lot, but
you know, considering.

00:45:09.490 --> 00:45:14.910
So soon we can sort of
start trade carbon sort of.

00:45:14.910 --> 00:45:18.120
Now so the idea is obviously
that every fifth person

00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:20.250
in the world doesn't
have access to energy.

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:22.120
And this is really
the perspective.

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:26.390
And we are already--

00:45:26.390 --> 00:45:29.820
we are close to finishing
a mobile phone charger.

00:45:29.820 --> 00:45:32.910
We have worked and worked
again on our business model.

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:36.390
We are close to breaking
a kind of investment

00:45:36.390 --> 00:45:38.970
into our little company here.

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:44.010
And so there's a number
of things going on.

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:45.570
The business model
is basically based

00:45:45.570 --> 00:45:49.410
on selling at a higher price
in places where there is power,

00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:52.560
and then we sell it at a lower
price where there is no power.

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:54.190
But we are market driven.

00:45:54.190 --> 00:45:56.320
It's not an aid project.

00:45:56.320 --> 00:46:00.360
It's not a, sort of, it's
supposed to build up--

00:46:00.360 --> 00:46:03.240
and that's a whole field
of itself, microeconomics.

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:06.360
To be at the bottom of pyramid
economical infrastructure,

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:08.490
and build up in
the retail system.

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:13.260
The whole idea of retailing
this is a whole field of itself.

00:46:13.260 --> 00:46:15.800
We have a little
task force in Berlin,

00:46:15.800 --> 00:46:18.980
where people are actually
being paid from the money being

00:46:18.980 --> 00:46:19.910
generated by this.

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:23.660
10 people are working now, and
a few people, and hopefully

00:46:23.660 --> 00:46:28.160
more and more people are
working in rural places, where

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:30.230
there's no access to energy.

00:46:30.230 --> 00:46:32.880
So we're very ambitious.

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:33.380
Hi.

00:46:33.380 --> 00:46:35.840
My name's Alana, and I'm a
first year graduate student here

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:36.680
at MIT Sloan.

00:46:36.680 --> 00:46:39.592
And you sold this product
across different geographies,

00:46:39.592 --> 00:46:41.300
and so I'd like to
know a little bit more

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:44.810
about how you've adapted your
messaging, or your marketing

00:46:44.810 --> 00:46:47.510
strategy, or your
dissemination to adapt

00:46:47.510 --> 00:46:49.760
to different geographies
and cultures.

00:46:49.760 --> 00:46:52.460
I think that's a tough one.

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:55.070
I don't think
we're that far yet.

00:46:55.070 --> 00:47:00.620
So the truth is that we actually
haven't picked our markets

00:47:00.620 --> 00:47:02.130
very strategically.

00:47:02.130 --> 00:47:06.310
The markets kind
of picked us up.

00:47:06.310 --> 00:47:09.940
When we started out, we knew
that, in order to succeed,

00:47:09.940 --> 00:47:15.310
we needed to focus, and then
learn from that experience,

00:47:15.310 --> 00:47:17.970
and spread that.

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:20.230
But we are also
about communication,

00:47:20.230 --> 00:47:22.560
and it is a global challenge.

00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:27.450
And in a political world,
having a global challenge,

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:29.250
and a global
communication platform,

00:47:29.250 --> 00:47:32.640
and only addressing the
challenge in one market,

00:47:32.640 --> 00:47:34.440
it doesn't work that well.

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:41.490
And Olafur and I are also both
incredibly bad at saying no.

00:47:41.490 --> 00:47:43.560
So when people ask
us to do something,

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:46.930
we tend to say yes, at
least from time to time.

00:47:46.930 --> 00:47:51.240
So a lot of the time, and
in business, and in markets,

00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:54.240
and selling is also about
meeting the right people

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:55.630
at the right time.

00:47:55.630 --> 00:47:59.160
So when we have felt
that somebody came along

00:47:59.160 --> 00:48:03.370
in a specific market wanted
to do something with this,

00:48:03.370 --> 00:48:07.210
we've said yes some times,
perhaps too many times.

00:48:07.210 --> 00:48:11.430
And that means we
actually haven't

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:16.410
made our strategic marketing
fit a specific market.

00:48:16.410 --> 00:48:18.210
We only have one
way of doing it,

00:48:18.210 --> 00:48:20.460
and we do the same in
Ethiopia, and Senegal,

00:48:20.460 --> 00:48:24.870
and Rwanda, Uganda,
Nairobi, the whole thing.

00:48:24.870 --> 00:48:27.430
And of course, that's not right.

00:48:27.430 --> 00:48:30.150
And we also know that, even
though the lamp is designed

00:48:30.150 --> 00:48:34.890
for Ethiopia, had the
design been made for Uganda,

00:48:34.890 --> 00:48:36.930
it would have been
different because that's

00:48:36.930 --> 00:48:38.023
how the nature is.

00:48:38.023 --> 00:48:39.690
So we also know that,
of course, there's

00:48:39.690 --> 00:48:42.653
a need for different marketing
to optimize your sale.

00:48:42.653 --> 00:48:44.820
But right now, we are at a
point where we are really

00:48:44.820 --> 00:48:46.590
happy that we're just selling.

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:50.130
To get into the optimization
of selling, we haven't done.

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:54.030
And I also think in that
whole marketing question,

00:48:54.030 --> 00:48:56.610
there's a really
interesting observation.

00:48:56.610 --> 00:49:00.280
Do you really need to market,
and why do you do marketing?

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:03.120
You normally tend to do
marketing, at least in the US,

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:05.910
if you want to sell products
that people don't want.

00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:09.240
So you need to tell them
to want the products.

00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:12.000
We don't need to do that
because our customers,

00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:13.320
they want the product.

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:15.900
So the biggest
challenge that has

00:49:15.900 --> 00:49:17.730
nothing to do with
Little Sun-- the biggest

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:23.130
challenge in solar devices
in Africa is delivery.

00:49:23.130 --> 00:49:24.450
It's not designing.

00:49:24.450 --> 00:49:25.620
It's not producing.

00:49:25.620 --> 00:49:26.520
It's not selling.

00:49:26.520 --> 00:49:28.120
It's delivery.

00:49:28.120 --> 00:49:30.900
So it's the supply chain
is actually the biggest

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:32.520
challenge for all of us.

00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:36.870
And we meet once a year at
conferences, and exchange

00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:39.600
knowledge, and it's the
same across the board.

00:49:39.600 --> 00:49:43.050
Everybody agrees that that is
actually the real challenge.

00:49:43.050 --> 00:49:47.520
D.light is the biggest player
on the solar appliance market.

00:49:50.280 --> 00:49:52.992
And you tend to think
they do radio marketing.

00:49:52.992 --> 00:49:55.200
So they're the only ones
that right now have a budget

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:58.320
to do radio marketing, and
it's fantastic for everybody.

00:49:58.320 --> 00:50:01.440
Because the customers, they
see a solar powered lamp,

00:50:01.440 --> 00:50:04.080
and to them that's a
d.light because d.light

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:05.340
is so strong in the marketing.

00:50:05.340 --> 00:50:08.627
So they created the Walkman,
and when people bought a Walkman

00:50:08.627 --> 00:50:10.710
in the 80s, they didn't
care if it came from Sony,

00:50:10.710 --> 00:50:12.418
or somebody else, it
was still a Walkman.

00:50:12.418 --> 00:50:14.590
And so a d.light is just
a solar powered lamp,

00:50:14.590 --> 00:50:17.292
so it benefits everybody.

00:50:17.292 --> 00:50:18.750
I think you did
answer my question.

00:50:18.750 --> 00:50:20.430
Thank you.

00:50:20.430 --> 00:50:22.710
I wanted to add
something with regard

00:50:22.710 --> 00:50:27.240
to the whole communication also
has to do with the awareness,

00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:31.350
and what footprint do
we then leave in people.

00:50:31.350 --> 00:50:37.920
And the awareness building
really is both on a--

00:50:37.920 --> 00:50:42.750
a child who grows up with this
reading every night in bed.

00:50:42.750 --> 00:50:45.840
How does that child make
a decision with regards

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:48.420
to energy, when that
child is grown up,

00:50:48.420 --> 00:50:53.610
and has to build a house,
and make a choice for energy?

00:50:53.610 --> 00:50:54.720
So there's that story.

00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:58.230
There's also the general
policy making, and the need

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:01.960
to address systems and
solutions, and so on.

00:51:01.960 --> 00:51:03.840
And one of the
challenges I think

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:08.530
is, again as we spoke
about, thinking and doing.

00:51:08.530 --> 00:51:11.430
And so I worked with
on a few occasions

00:51:11.430 --> 00:51:15.930
with Little Sun and our team,
and for like with on how

00:51:15.930 --> 00:51:18.840
to communicate the
lamp in other ways

00:51:18.840 --> 00:51:20.250
than the traditional marketing.

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:23.430
And obviously, on a large
scale, it doesn't work.

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:27.060
This is a picture of a project
that we are doing occasionally,

00:51:27.060 --> 00:51:29.190
and I think we are
now doing it on the--

00:51:29.190 --> 00:51:30.480
what is it called, Casilla?

00:51:30.480 --> 00:51:33.580
Here in America,
the music festival?

00:51:33.580 --> 00:51:35.340
Coachella.

00:51:35.340 --> 00:51:38.280
So Coachella, they love
us, and we love them.

00:51:38.280 --> 00:51:39.900
So it's a happy marriage.

00:51:39.900 --> 00:51:42.330
And so they ask, why
don't you come and do

00:51:42.330 --> 00:51:44.320
some sort of Little Sun stuff?

00:51:44.320 --> 00:51:45.300
So this is in a way.

00:51:45.300 --> 00:51:47.130
And we could do it also--

00:51:47.130 --> 00:51:49.650
we're trying to build
a music festival,

00:51:49.650 --> 00:51:52.830
a sort of block party in Addis
Ababa, and this sort of thing.

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:54.870
So the idea is that
you make a graffiti,

00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:57.330
or a drawing with a lamp.

00:51:57.330 --> 00:52:01.650
And interestingly, I
think this idea of motion

00:52:01.650 --> 00:52:05.235
is about the difference
between just thinking about, oh

00:52:05.235 --> 00:52:06.360
this is really interesting.

00:52:06.360 --> 00:52:07.320
You might touch it.

00:52:07.320 --> 00:52:08.320
It's a good beginning.

00:52:08.320 --> 00:52:13.470
But if you start to dance,
then you are, one could say,

00:52:13.470 --> 00:52:15.175
you are a little
bit traumatized.

00:52:15.175 --> 00:52:16.800
You know what I mean
when I said before

00:52:16.800 --> 00:52:18.258
that you kind of have memory?

00:52:18.258 --> 00:52:19.800
Because then you go
home, and you go,

00:52:19.800 --> 00:52:21.270
oh yeah that was that lamp.

00:52:21.270 --> 00:52:25.080
You know, now I
know how it feels.

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:31.260
So you store this
story through motion.

00:52:31.260 --> 00:52:35.460
And as we know from, and that
apparently came from MIT,

00:52:35.460 --> 00:52:38.100
if you make gymnastics
by learning mathematics,

00:52:38.100 --> 00:52:40.560
you're much more likely
to remember it long term.

00:52:40.560 --> 00:52:42.240
You know, so all
the kind of stories

00:52:42.240 --> 00:52:45.030
of knowledge and movement,
and it's a big mistake

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:48.000
that you're all sitting
down like this with regards

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:48.660
to what we say.

00:52:48.660 --> 00:52:50.250
It's simply not going to be--

00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:51.960
you should be
jumping up and down.

00:52:51.960 --> 00:52:55.680
So the point-- maybe you
can turn on the globe.

00:52:55.680 --> 00:52:58.150
So this photo, as I said,
is me trying to draw,

00:52:58.150 --> 00:53:02.370
and unsuccessfully
trying to write something

00:53:02.370 --> 00:53:04.410
in front of a sort of
stop motion camera, which

00:53:04.410 --> 00:53:05.670
captures the light.

00:53:05.670 --> 00:53:08.922
But essentially,
the project brings

00:53:08.922 --> 00:53:10.380
the Coachella, for
instance, and we

00:53:10.380 --> 00:53:11.760
have done it again and again.

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:16.110
It then sends that picture
onto an online planet,

00:53:16.110 --> 00:53:19.650
and this planet is, which is
then built. But so you see,

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:21.690
so it's like kind
of building a sun.

00:53:21.690 --> 00:53:23.640
So essentially, this
is, at some point,

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:26.280
going to be so full that
it's just because the sun.

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:28.770
When you Zoom into it,
you'll see all the signatures

00:53:28.770 --> 00:53:31.800
of the people, and the
signatures are kind of a dance.

00:53:31.800 --> 00:53:34.470
So it's just a little
marketing project,

00:53:34.470 --> 00:53:35.970
which is about
awareness building,

00:53:35.970 --> 00:53:38.280
and creating a sense of
community, and sharing the sun,

00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:38.780
and--

00:53:38.780 --> 00:53:41.580
so there is plenty of small
little town projects like this,

00:53:41.580 --> 00:53:45.180
which reach out into
what we call social media

00:53:45.180 --> 00:53:48.930
type of Twitter friendly stuff.

00:53:48.930 --> 00:53:53.430
But it also addresses the fact,
well, understanding energy

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:55.710
is not so easy because
energy is very abstract,

00:53:55.710 --> 00:53:58.560
except for incredible
people like you here at MIT.

00:53:58.560 --> 00:54:02.475
But generally speaking, energy
is something very difficult.

00:54:02.475 --> 00:54:05.280
It's very difficult to
understand the climate crisis.

00:54:05.280 --> 00:54:08.050
Oh my God, you know,
it's so intangible.

00:54:11.110 --> 00:54:11.740
Hi.

00:54:11.740 --> 00:54:12.700
My name is Bertha.

00:54:12.700 --> 00:54:16.870
I'm a PhD student
doing bar electronics.

00:54:16.870 --> 00:54:20.680
My question is more towards
the merging of the two markets.

00:54:20.680 --> 00:54:23.290
So you're developing this
product for the developing

00:54:23.290 --> 00:54:26.350
world, people without
access to electricity,

00:54:26.350 --> 00:54:30.250
and a lot of the marketing, and
the buzz that you're creating

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:32.800
is in the developed world,
with the social media

00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:34.880
platforms and exhibitions.

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:37.840
So in your product,
how important

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:45.610
is that, and is that how most
of the money and the buzz

00:54:45.610 --> 00:54:47.590
is being generated?

00:54:47.590 --> 00:54:51.610
And just leading on
to that, how important

00:54:51.610 --> 00:54:55.960
do you think, in general, kind
of trying to merge these two

00:54:55.960 --> 00:54:59.020
markets is, rather
than thinking of this

00:54:59.020 --> 00:55:05.320
as the thing that the developing
world as the consumers,

00:55:05.320 --> 00:55:08.680
and getting money from
the developed world?

00:55:11.900 --> 00:55:16.690
I think one of the good things,
with especially social media,

00:55:16.690 --> 00:55:20.050
is social media, they don't
have a Facebook for Africa,

00:55:20.050 --> 00:55:21.880
and Facebook for
the United States.

00:55:21.880 --> 00:55:23.360
They only have a Facebook.

00:55:23.360 --> 00:55:27.520
So when you use social
media, it works equally well

00:55:27.520 --> 00:55:29.240
on all our markets.

00:55:29.240 --> 00:55:31.060
And I think if we
had tons of money,

00:55:31.060 --> 00:55:35.020
and wanted to market the Little
Sun really heavily in Nigeria,

00:55:35.020 --> 00:55:37.030
the most effective
thing would probably

00:55:37.030 --> 00:55:40.090
be a huge big poster on
Fifth Avenue in New York.

00:55:40.090 --> 00:55:44.920
And so most people don't realize
that the globe is unbelievably

00:55:44.920 --> 00:55:47.350
small, and that we
all see the same,

00:55:47.350 --> 00:55:50.090
and that we all
aspire for the same.

00:55:50.090 --> 00:55:53.380
So when we do a
big thing in Milan,

00:55:53.380 --> 00:55:56.110
that's hugely
important in Zimbabwe.

00:55:56.110 --> 00:55:57.310
And we know that.

00:55:57.310 --> 00:56:01.000
So we have people in
small stores in Zimbabwe

00:56:01.000 --> 00:56:06.140
saying that it is so fantastic
that we do stuff in Milan.

00:56:06.140 --> 00:56:07.660
So yeah, I can add to that.

00:56:07.660 --> 00:56:10.270
A good example is a Renaissance
is a shopping mall in Milan

00:56:10.270 --> 00:56:12.730
in front of the Duomo, the
most expensive shopping

00:56:12.730 --> 00:56:16.660
mall for the kind of-- so
they gave us the Christmas

00:56:16.660 --> 00:56:19.960
windows Little Sun Project,
that project, stuff like that.

00:56:19.960 --> 00:56:21.460
So very interestingly,
we were doing

00:56:21.460 --> 00:56:24.220
a pilot kiosk in Addis Ababa.

00:56:24.220 --> 00:56:26.222
Assefa-- maybe you can
put Assefa on there.

00:56:26.222 --> 00:56:27.430
It's the one with the spread.

00:56:27.430 --> 00:56:31.480
So Assefa-- we said, ah you know
Christmas and Milan, we'll go,

00:56:31.480 --> 00:56:34.390
and then we do a Christmas
party, a Little Sun Christmas

00:56:34.390 --> 00:56:35.380
party.

00:56:35.380 --> 00:56:37.240
Then Assefa says, oh amazing.

00:56:37.240 --> 00:56:38.972
Oh I love Milan.

00:56:38.972 --> 00:56:39.680
Milan is amazing.

00:56:39.680 --> 00:56:40.240
I love Milan.

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:43.360
I'm selling something;
Milan is selling something.

00:56:43.360 --> 00:56:45.160
We're in the same boat.

00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:45.730
It's great.

00:56:45.730 --> 00:56:46.420
I'm going to go.

00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:47.500
That's Assefa.

00:56:47.500 --> 00:56:50.350
So Assefa says, I'm going to
go to Milan for the Christmas

00:56:50.350 --> 00:56:52.900
party, but the truth is
he's not because he's

00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:54.400
selling cigarettes
out of the packs,

00:56:54.400 --> 00:56:56.140
and chewing gum one
piece at a time.

00:56:56.140 --> 00:56:58.870
But he was like so into
the party, totally loved

00:56:58.870 --> 00:57:01.060
it, easy going, easy peasy.

00:57:01.060 --> 00:57:03.010
Then we are in Milan
at the Christmas party,

00:57:03.010 --> 00:57:04.810
doing a little bit of
sort of preparation,

00:57:04.810 --> 00:57:09.430
and so on, communication prep
with the team, the sales team,

00:57:09.430 --> 00:57:10.820
and so on, and so forth.

00:57:10.820 --> 00:57:15.280
And then, of course, everybody
goes, oh Assefa, the guy,

00:57:15.280 --> 00:57:18.340
we're going to help us, so
we will party for Assefa.

00:57:18.340 --> 00:57:21.220
So the idea that they
were partying with Assefa

00:57:21.220 --> 00:57:22.330
was really not present.

00:57:22.330 --> 00:57:24.610
So the distance from
Addis Ababa to Milan

00:57:24.610 --> 00:57:27.130
is much shorter than from
Milan to Addis Ababa.

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:29.950
So the mindset is
very interesting.

00:57:29.950 --> 00:57:32.260
The mindset is, with
regards to the matter,

00:57:32.260 --> 00:57:34.180
there's a huge difference
in the conception,

00:57:34.180 --> 00:57:36.670
the kind of interpretation
of the African market

00:57:36.670 --> 00:57:38.860
from the kind of northern.

00:57:38.860 --> 00:57:45.610
And normally, I would always say
we are five years behind what

00:57:45.610 --> 00:57:48.160
is actually going on.

00:57:48.160 --> 00:57:50.630
And of course, it's a
little more complex.

00:57:50.630 --> 00:57:51.730
And of course, it is--

00:57:51.730 --> 00:57:56.410
I mean we have trained to
stop saying underdeveloped,

00:57:56.410 --> 00:57:58.000
developing, developed.

00:57:58.000 --> 00:57:59.680
We've just trained
to stop saying it

00:57:59.680 --> 00:58:01.412
because it's so
counterproductive.

00:58:01.412 --> 00:58:03.370
When you are in Addis
Ababa, and say, oh you're

00:58:03.370 --> 00:58:04.998
sort of underdeveloped,
Assefa goes,

00:58:04.998 --> 00:58:06.540
what do you mean
it's underdeveloped?

00:58:06.540 --> 00:58:07.415
It's not undeveloped.

00:58:07.415 --> 00:58:09.280
It's a very good kiosk.

00:58:09.280 --> 00:58:13.180
So it's been a learning curve.

00:58:13.180 --> 00:58:19.710
And I think the truth
is we should also not

00:58:19.710 --> 00:58:21.720
be afraid of saying
whether sometimes it

00:58:21.720 --> 00:58:23.220
is bloody underdeveloped.

00:58:23.220 --> 00:58:25.050
You know, girls
don't go to school.

00:58:25.050 --> 00:58:26.550
That is underdeveloped.

00:58:26.550 --> 00:58:30.840
And to find a balance, and sort
of a way of addressing this

00:58:30.840 --> 00:58:36.370
without being excluded, or
exclusive, or something.

00:58:36.370 --> 00:58:37.530
It's a complex challenge.

00:58:37.530 --> 00:58:41.760
But by all means, a
lot of the challenges

00:58:41.760 --> 00:58:45.710
are very much in the
northern hemisphere,

00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:47.880
and much to a much
lesser extent,

00:58:47.880 --> 00:58:50.370
in the southern hemisphere.

00:58:50.370 --> 00:58:53.790
I think now, I just remembered
a funny story with regards

00:58:53.790 --> 00:58:56.100
to marketing and technology.

00:58:56.100 --> 00:58:59.220
I think now that we
are here in Teta.

00:58:59.220 --> 00:59:01.740
And engineering,
so you're all aware

00:59:01.740 --> 00:59:04.890
that you made the
Nano a phenomenal car

00:59:04.890 --> 00:59:06.780
from an engineering perspective.

00:59:06.780 --> 00:59:09.540
I mean, they made an
achievement that no car company

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:11.850
had made so far.

00:59:11.850 --> 00:59:14.430
They marketed the cheapest
car in the world, which

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:16.320
is an astonishing achievement.

00:59:16.320 --> 00:59:17.915
But who wants that?

00:59:17.915 --> 00:59:19.290
Do you think it
became a success?

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:20.430
No, it did not.

00:59:20.430 --> 00:59:23.790
Nobody wants to have the
cheapest car in the world.

00:59:23.790 --> 00:59:27.610
Now they're repositioning
it with huge costs.

00:59:27.610 --> 00:59:29.760
So if you make
something-- and this,

00:59:29.760 --> 00:59:34.930
right now, is the cheapest solar
powered lamp on the market.

00:59:34.930 --> 00:59:39.030
So going back to d.light, their
cheapest version is called S1,

00:59:39.030 --> 00:59:43.140
and that retails in Uganda
between $12 and $14.

00:59:43.140 --> 00:59:46.050
We retail in Uganda
between $10 and $12,

00:59:46.050 --> 00:59:48.210
which is slightly below.

00:59:48.210 --> 00:59:51.000
We are the cheapest
lamp on the market.

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:52.980
We also think we're
the most beautiful.

00:59:52.980 --> 00:59:56.238
Do we ever tell anybody
that we are the cheapest

00:59:56.238 --> 00:59:57.030
lamp on the market?

00:59:57.030 --> 00:59:58.110
Of course not.

00:59:58.110 --> 01:00:00.210
We tell them that we are
the most beautiful lamp

01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:03.910
on the market because we think
that's what our customers want.

01:00:03.910 --> 01:00:06.180
So that's, again, how
do you communicate?

01:00:06.180 --> 01:00:10.860
And that communication
translates very well globally.

01:00:10.860 --> 01:00:13.812
I was in a class a little
while ago, and it's D-Lab.

01:00:13.812 --> 01:00:15.270
I don't know if
you've heard of it.

01:00:15.270 --> 01:00:18.510
They do a lot of products
in countries, like Africa,

01:00:18.510 --> 01:00:20.070
and South America, and so forth.

01:00:20.070 --> 01:00:21.510
And one of the things
I had asked them about

01:00:21.510 --> 01:00:22.968
was the fact that
there's a million

01:00:22.968 --> 01:00:24.870
and seven different
kinds of solar lamps,

01:00:24.870 --> 01:00:28.692
and why despite that, there
still seems to be this problem.

01:00:28.692 --> 01:00:30.900
One of the things they
brought up was the Little Sun,

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:33.690
and they said, that in
their experience traveling,

01:00:33.690 --> 01:00:36.270
it was seen more as a toy
than an actual appliance.

01:00:36.270 --> 01:00:37.770
Now, I don't know
if that's actually

01:00:37.770 --> 01:00:39.870
a good thing or a bad thing
because kids are still

01:00:39.870 --> 01:00:42.240
using it, but what are
your thoughts on that?

01:00:42.240 --> 01:00:44.410
I think that's a plus.

01:00:44.410 --> 01:00:48.090
So if you know that
your consumer group is

01:00:48.090 --> 01:00:54.180
below 16, and perceiving it as
a toy, then you hit your market.

01:00:54.180 --> 01:00:59.340
I also think when they say
they perceive it as a toy,

01:00:59.340 --> 01:01:02.940
it's probably that it's very
optimistic, and slightly naive.

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:06.510
And children and toys
tend to be that way.

01:01:06.510 --> 01:01:10.170
I haven't experienced
anybody not liking it,

01:01:10.170 --> 01:01:12.160
and not smiling
when they saw it.

01:01:12.160 --> 01:01:15.640
So one thing is that you
perceive it as a toy,

01:01:15.640 --> 01:01:17.910
but you quickly
realize that it works.

01:01:17.910 --> 01:01:21.570
The main downside
on kerosene lantern,

01:01:21.570 --> 01:01:26.940
which is a bio-fuel
lantern light

01:01:26.940 --> 01:01:32.370
is the health issues,
especially for the ones

01:01:32.370 --> 01:01:34.980
who sits right next to
the kerosene lantern.

01:01:34.980 --> 01:01:39.030
A single wick kerosene lantern,
if you sit within a meter

01:01:39.030 --> 01:01:41.310
distance, it's the
equivalent in two hours

01:01:41.310 --> 01:01:44.470
to smoking 40 cigarettes.

01:01:44.470 --> 01:01:46.410
And this means, if
you are a child doing

01:01:46.410 --> 01:01:49.140
your homework, which
is the most exposed

01:01:49.140 --> 01:01:53.100
in the household because they
literally sit-in, like you

01:01:53.100 --> 01:01:55.500
know, they burn their
front hair on the kerosene

01:01:55.500 --> 01:01:59.370
because they sit with a lamp,
and the kerosene, and the book.

01:01:59.370 --> 01:02:04.350
So this means that the kind
of major instant impact

01:02:04.350 --> 01:02:06.180
is a health impact.

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:10.050
The more long term impact is
hopefully generating a cheaper

01:02:10.050 --> 01:02:12.360
chain of economy, bringing
a little more resources

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:14.490
to the families simply
because they are buying,

01:02:14.490 --> 01:02:16.450
over the lifespan
of three years--

01:02:16.450 --> 01:02:19.020
which is the rechargeable
batteries in this--

01:02:19.020 --> 01:02:23.310
that will allow for a
little bit extra money

01:02:23.310 --> 01:02:26.070
to maybe keep one
child out of labor,

01:02:26.070 --> 01:02:28.470
and put that one child
into school maybe.

01:02:28.470 --> 01:02:30.720
And so there is a
number of benefits,

01:02:30.720 --> 01:02:33.780
but they all somehow
involves children.

01:02:38.700 --> 01:02:40.950
Typically, the people
in the household,

01:02:40.950 --> 01:02:43.110
as far as we have
come to understand,

01:02:43.110 --> 01:02:49.020
who spent money on this type
of material is also the mother.

01:02:51.570 --> 01:02:55.260
Basically, in our system, the
father's out of the equation.

01:02:55.260 --> 01:02:59.880
He just would never
buy a solar lantern.

01:02:59.880 --> 01:03:02.850
So this is a bit of a
follow up on their behavior.

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:06.270
So seeing as the purpose
of the Little Sun

01:03:06.270 --> 01:03:08.880
is to actually
have an experience

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:11.580
kind of with the
individual that's using it,

01:03:11.580 --> 01:03:13.980
what has been your
follow up with people

01:03:13.980 --> 01:03:15.060
who have purchased this?

01:03:15.060 --> 01:03:17.850
Have you seen them
using it as a toy?

01:03:17.850 --> 01:03:20.700
Do literacy in regions
increase with this?

01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:23.070
I guess have you measured
some kind of behavior trends

01:03:23.070 --> 01:03:24.660
on the other side?

01:03:24.660 --> 01:03:26.310
We don't have this.

01:03:26.310 --> 01:03:28.950
We just a small
start up, so we don't

01:03:28.950 --> 01:03:31.500
have the capabilities of
going out and measuring

01:03:31.500 --> 01:03:33.630
the results of the Little Sun.

01:03:33.630 --> 01:03:38.100
We rely on the World Bank
and Lighting Africa studies

01:03:38.100 --> 01:03:41.040
on what happens in
environments where you

01:03:41.040 --> 01:03:42.840
introduce solar powered light.

01:03:42.840 --> 01:03:46.440
And those studies always
show that you actually

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:50.310
have an increase in studying
hours, and also an increase

01:03:50.310 --> 01:03:53.850
in education
learning on that one.

01:03:53.850 --> 01:03:55.860
But it's not a Little
Sun thing, it's

01:03:55.860 --> 01:03:58.080
a solar powered lamp thing.

01:03:58.080 --> 01:04:00.630
And we also, I think we
learned a little bit-- oh

01:04:00.630 --> 01:04:02.970
I'm getting louder and
louder, or is that just me?

01:04:02.970 --> 01:04:03.720
Hey.

01:04:03.720 --> 01:04:06.090
So what is so important--

01:04:06.090 --> 01:04:10.060
but what we also see is
actually it's hard to--

01:04:10.060 --> 01:04:11.490
we should be a
little bit careful

01:04:11.490 --> 01:04:15.180
to predict too much because we
were with a-- you were with a,

01:04:15.180 --> 01:04:18.580
I believe, an
elderly gentleman who

01:04:18.580 --> 01:04:20.340
was going to go out
every morning, 4

01:04:20.340 --> 01:04:22.410
o'clock in the morning,
to milk the cow.

01:04:22.410 --> 01:04:24.870
And you said, as I said
to him, oh why don't you

01:04:24.870 --> 01:04:26.170
use the Little Sun?

01:04:26.170 --> 01:04:28.850
It's about 4:00 in the
morning; it's dark.

01:04:28.850 --> 01:04:32.070
He said, no, no,
I've been doing that

01:04:32.070 --> 01:04:34.170
in darkness for 40 years or so.

01:04:34.170 --> 01:04:36.180
I kind of know how that goes.

01:04:36.180 --> 01:04:39.180
But you know, now I've
become a little older,

01:04:39.180 --> 01:04:41.250
what I'm afraid of
now is actually,

01:04:41.250 --> 01:04:43.560
when I go to the
toilet by night--

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:45.360
which he didn't want
to talk about really--

01:04:45.360 --> 01:04:47.670
I'm afraid of falling,
and if I break a hip,

01:04:47.670 --> 01:04:48.840
my family falls apart.

01:04:51.870 --> 01:04:54.570
So it was unpredictable
the kind of use.

01:04:54.570 --> 01:04:57.210
And of course, we
have seen some people

01:04:57.210 --> 01:04:59.805
who just sort of use
it to kind of party,

01:04:59.805 --> 01:05:02.520
or just sort of have fun.

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:05.010
Because we are so, oh you
have to educate the children,

01:05:05.010 --> 01:05:07.135
but then the children's
just kind of playing around

01:05:07.135 --> 01:05:08.160
with it as a toy.

01:05:08.160 --> 01:05:12.030
Kind of like not doing
their homework, damn it.

01:05:12.030 --> 01:05:16.920
So some of it also has to
do with that it's just--

01:05:16.920 --> 01:05:20.730
there's a certain limit to
how to define the success.

01:05:20.730 --> 01:05:25.800
I wonder why do we need an
artist in order to solve this,

01:05:25.800 --> 01:05:29.010
or to find the answer
for these problems?

01:05:29.010 --> 01:05:33.990
And of course, I
know and all we know

01:05:33.990 --> 01:05:35.850
that you are not working alone.

01:05:35.850 --> 01:05:37.360
You work with a group.

01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:44.780
But why not an economist,
an engineer, an architect,

01:05:44.780 --> 01:05:48.920
a sociologist, or a politician?

01:05:48.920 --> 01:05:50.100
That's a good question.

01:05:50.100 --> 01:05:52.700
I actually also don't
know whether I am actually

01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:54.410
an artist in that way.

01:05:54.410 --> 01:06:00.920
I don't, to be honest,
think of myself as an artist

01:06:00.920 --> 01:06:02.060
in order to do something.

01:06:02.060 --> 01:06:06.140
I do think of me as a
part of a civic system,

01:06:06.140 --> 01:06:08.930
and being a contributor,
or a participant,

01:06:08.930 --> 01:06:11.840
or coproducer of a civic system.

01:06:11.840 --> 01:06:14.510
I see that I bring
in my skill set,

01:06:14.510 --> 01:06:17.670
but I actually don't think
you need an artist at all,

01:06:17.670 --> 01:06:18.350
to be honest.

01:06:18.350 --> 01:06:22.190
And as we know, there is
plenty of success stories

01:06:22.190 --> 01:06:26.370
in the world, sadly, where
there is no artist involved.

01:06:26.370 --> 01:06:28.685
So I don't think one
can say by definition--

01:06:31.480 --> 01:06:32.770
I do think, though, that--

01:06:32.770 --> 01:06:34.720
I could not end it there.

01:06:34.720 --> 01:06:41.260
So I do think that art, frankly
speaking, it is, especially

01:06:41.260 --> 01:06:48.430
today, as success
is increasingly

01:06:48.430 --> 01:06:50.850
being defined by
quantifiable measures,

01:06:50.850 --> 01:06:55.660
the definition of success is
becoming narrower and narrower.

01:06:55.660 --> 01:06:58.570
If it's not
sellable, measurable,

01:06:58.570 --> 01:07:02.620
if you don't become rich,
it's probably not a success.

01:07:02.620 --> 01:07:05.620
And sadly, a lot of
soft skill successes,

01:07:05.620 --> 01:07:08.950
unpredictable or
soft successes are

01:07:08.950 --> 01:07:13.720
kind of falling off the cliff in
this deep, competitive, market

01:07:13.720 --> 01:07:18.440
driven, arrogant, probabilistic
sort of world we live in.

01:07:18.440 --> 01:07:22.360
The lack of interdependence,
the lack of altruistic effort,

01:07:22.360 --> 01:07:25.090
and all of that is
simply kind of-- you

01:07:25.090 --> 01:07:27.520
don't have a politician
who comes around

01:07:27.520 --> 01:07:30.940
to say, well, as long as the
art museum shows good art,

01:07:30.940 --> 01:07:31.770
I'm happy.

01:07:31.770 --> 01:07:33.520
They would always say,
well, if it doesn't

01:07:33.520 --> 01:07:36.610
have 200,000 people
a year, probably

01:07:36.610 --> 01:07:38.300
it's then not a good art museum.

01:07:38.300 --> 01:07:40.540
Of course, we all know
that art has nothing

01:07:40.540 --> 01:07:43.360
to do with the amount of
people, but the politicians just

01:07:43.360 --> 01:07:44.770
won't get elected.

01:07:44.770 --> 01:07:47.710
So this means, by all means--

01:07:47.710 --> 01:07:50.620
this is not artist, but
there is a lot of things

01:07:50.620 --> 01:07:56.810
that I would argue we need
to start to treasure what

01:07:56.810 --> 01:07:58.750
creativity is capable of.

01:07:58.750 --> 01:08:02.930
And artists are certainly not
the only ones who are creative.

01:08:02.930 --> 01:08:06.157
We argue all of
[INAUDIBLE],, I mean.

01:08:06.157 --> 01:08:08.240
But just generally speaking,
we should really just

01:08:08.240 --> 01:08:10.830
be very careful.

01:08:10.830 --> 01:08:16.010
And this is why I would
suggest if now we're

01:08:16.010 --> 01:08:19.279
sort of in an engineering,
under your great guidance,

01:08:19.279 --> 01:08:21.410
and then into
engineering environment,

01:08:21.410 --> 01:08:23.750
I really encourage
and suggest that you

01:08:23.750 --> 01:08:27.350
would reach out, and ask
artists, and really ask

01:08:27.350 --> 01:08:28.399
creative people.

01:08:28.399 --> 01:08:29.870
And that might slow you down.

01:08:29.870 --> 01:08:31.130
It might take you on a detour.

01:08:31.130 --> 01:08:33.380
It might actually pose
a problem that you

01:08:33.380 --> 01:08:35.090
think is not important.

01:08:35.090 --> 01:08:39.359
But generally speaking, I
really think it's worth it.

01:08:39.359 --> 01:08:42.830
You're also suggesting that
when we run our conference

01:08:42.830 --> 01:08:46.460
on our work, we invite a very
diverse audience, including

01:08:46.460 --> 01:08:50.300
artists, to just listen
to what they have to say.

01:08:50.300 --> 01:08:54.890
Yes, I mean, as much as
I can celebrate artists,

01:08:54.890 --> 01:08:58.314
there's no doubt that artists
are elitist, arrogant.

01:08:58.314 --> 01:09:00.439
Everything that I've been
talking about in society,

01:09:00.439 --> 01:09:02.180
that happens in art too, right?

01:09:02.180 --> 01:09:04.670
In museums, they're
sort of single minded,

01:09:04.670 --> 01:09:09.260
snobbish, like super
[? wide. ?] So there's so much

01:09:09.260 --> 01:09:11.899
problems in art, and the
solution is obviously a coming

01:09:11.899 --> 01:09:17.870
together from all sides.

01:09:17.870 --> 01:09:19.880
And I can tell you
that engineers are not

01:09:19.880 --> 01:09:23.037
that way, certainly not
in this institution.

01:09:23.037 --> 01:09:24.859
[LAUGHTER]

01:09:24.859 --> 01:09:26.640
Let's go to the next question.

01:09:26.640 --> 01:09:28.279
It comes from
Professor [? Shaya. ?]

01:09:28.279 --> 01:09:30.740
Some people in this
audience have come here

01:09:30.740 --> 01:09:33.560
from all the way from Mumbai.

01:09:33.560 --> 01:09:36.540
Yeah, I have two questions.

01:09:36.540 --> 01:09:38.960
One is a small question,
and the next question

01:09:38.960 --> 01:09:40.609
is a larger question.

01:09:40.609 --> 01:09:44.450
The small question is what
is the battery life in this?

01:09:44.450 --> 01:09:46.399
2 and 1/2 years.

01:09:46.399 --> 01:09:48.680
2 and 1/2 years is
the short reply.

01:09:48.680 --> 01:09:51.270
And the product life
is about 10 years?

01:09:51.270 --> 01:09:52.819
That would be quite fair to say.

01:09:52.819 --> 01:09:53.479
Yes.

01:09:53.479 --> 01:09:54.050
Right.

01:09:54.050 --> 01:09:54.550
OK.

01:09:54.550 --> 01:09:57.650
So now the larger
question is as follows.

01:09:57.650 --> 01:10:01.790
You're not like having
visited and surveyed

01:10:01.790 --> 01:10:05.897
the areas in Africa, and
developing countries--

01:10:05.897 --> 01:10:08.105
I'm glad you didn't call it
an underdeveloped country

01:10:08.105 --> 01:10:10.340
and developing country.

01:10:10.340 --> 01:10:13.250
So if you see the
development goals,

01:10:13.250 --> 01:10:18.320
one sees that any product
that sort of helps directly

01:10:18.320 --> 01:10:22.850
in poverty reduction
makes a larger impact.

01:10:22.850 --> 01:10:27.470
So your company like Little
Sun, if it makes success,

01:10:27.470 --> 01:10:30.770
and I'm sure it will, all
the best for your product,

01:10:30.770 --> 01:10:33.470
but what would be,
based on your survey,

01:10:33.470 --> 01:10:36.680
and visits to the
African countries,

01:10:36.680 --> 01:10:38.660
and other developing
countries, what

01:10:38.660 --> 01:10:41.870
could be a product
that would sort of help

01:10:41.870 --> 01:10:46.730
in the sort of
livelihood opportunities,

01:10:46.730 --> 01:10:50.300
so that poverty is
reduced, and some

01:10:50.300 --> 01:10:55.180
of the millennium development
goals are achieved?

01:10:55.180 --> 01:10:56.200
Did you get my question?

01:10:56.200 --> 01:10:57.640
You said what product--

01:10:57.640 --> 01:10:58.960
Which product would--

01:10:58.960 --> 01:11:02.740
But I certainly do think
that Little Sun participated

01:11:02.740 --> 01:11:03.970
in exactly that.

01:11:03.970 --> 01:11:08.080
We see in the environments
that when you buy a Little Sun,

01:11:08.080 --> 01:11:09.820
you're not spending
money, you're

01:11:09.820 --> 01:11:14.230
saving money because you
free up approximately 90%

01:11:14.230 --> 01:11:19.660
of your current expenditure on
kerosene, or oil, or paraffin,

01:11:19.660 --> 01:11:21.160
candles, whatever you do.

01:11:21.160 --> 01:11:23.800
So there is a cost
saving that you

01:11:23.800 --> 01:11:25.540
could spend for something else.

01:11:25.540 --> 01:11:29.170
You also see people making
a profit selling them.

01:11:29.170 --> 01:11:34.660
So as soon as the lamp
reaches our market,

01:11:34.660 --> 01:11:36.850
there's a normal
profit margin in it

01:11:36.850 --> 01:11:39.880
for everybody that moves
towards the customer.

01:11:39.880 --> 01:11:41.220
They make a profit.

01:11:41.220 --> 01:11:45.710
So that's profit
generated in that region.

01:11:45.710 --> 01:11:48.100
You have better
education, learning.

01:11:48.100 --> 01:11:51.010
As I said before, that
generates in turn,

01:11:51.010 --> 01:11:54.820
it'll probably take 10-15 years,
more profit, and more self

01:11:54.820 --> 01:11:56.300
esteem, and understanding.

01:11:56.300 --> 01:11:58.720
So there's a whole
array of things

01:11:58.720 --> 01:12:03.550
that happens when you introduce
small products like this.

01:12:03.550 --> 01:12:07.690
And that is, again, the same
with all similar products.

01:12:07.690 --> 01:12:11.920
We see a world of
interconnectivity.

01:12:11.920 --> 01:12:16.450
We see everybody in the
world with a cell phone.

01:12:16.450 --> 01:12:20.200
So giving people energy,
I mean subsidizing light,

01:12:20.200 --> 01:12:21.580
is relatively easy.

01:12:21.580 --> 01:12:24.400
So you could burn a little
bit of oil, or whatever

01:12:24.400 --> 01:12:27.160
you want to get light,
but it's pretty hard

01:12:27.160 --> 01:12:31.150
to charge your cell
phone with a candle.

01:12:31.150 --> 01:12:34.960
So subsidizing the
energy needed to supply

01:12:34.960 --> 01:12:36.820
energy for a smartphone,
or an iPhone,

01:12:36.820 --> 01:12:42.580
or that kind of appliances,
that's a natural next step.

01:12:42.580 --> 01:12:44.890
And these products are
coming out everywhere.

01:12:44.890 --> 01:12:48.790
I mean, we're seeing
smartphones just

01:12:48.790 --> 01:12:51.280
growing in the African market,
and in the Indian market,

01:12:51.280 --> 01:12:53.440
tremendously.

01:12:53.440 --> 01:12:56.350
Today, there is way more
cell phones in Africa

01:12:56.350 --> 01:12:58.060
than in Europe.

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:00.940
Right now, Europe is slightly
ahead on the smartphone market,

01:13:00.940 --> 01:13:04.470
but that will only
last maybe two years.

01:13:04.470 --> 01:13:06.830
So these are coming,
and that again,

01:13:06.830 --> 01:13:09.500
connects us all
completely, going back

01:13:09.500 --> 01:13:10.760
to the social media thing.

01:13:10.760 --> 01:13:13.220
So delivering the
energy to do that--

01:13:13.220 --> 01:13:16.670
and we are seeing education
platforms going out,

01:13:16.670 --> 01:13:20.540
and that kind of media
as well creating jobs.

01:13:20.540 --> 01:13:24.890
I mean, that really means that
you could live in a rural area,

01:13:24.890 --> 01:13:27.650
anywhere in the world,
delivering software

01:13:27.650 --> 01:13:30.830
to General Electrics,
just to give an example.

01:13:30.830 --> 01:13:32.750
Because we will be able
to work-- the reason

01:13:32.750 --> 01:13:37.170
they can't do that today
is because they don't have

01:13:37.170 --> 01:13:39.060
the computer, and they
don't have the energy

01:13:39.060 --> 01:13:40.320
to run the computer.

01:13:40.320 --> 01:13:45.510
Right now, in India, I could
buy a little laptop, $38,

01:13:45.510 --> 01:13:48.510
which is actually a sum
that most of our customers

01:13:48.510 --> 01:13:50.565
could come up with,
if they really wanted.

01:13:50.565 --> 01:13:52.440
But right now, they
can't run it because they

01:13:52.440 --> 01:13:53.610
don't have the internet.

01:13:53.610 --> 01:13:55.920
But if we can deliver
autonomous energy,

01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:59.160
and small digital
devices, then the world

01:13:59.160 --> 01:14:00.720
will truly come together.

01:14:00.720 --> 01:14:02.580
And I certainly
think that will help

01:14:02.580 --> 01:14:04.380
a lot of the millennium goals.

01:14:04.380 --> 01:14:07.740
The Little Sun project
feels like a side project.

01:14:07.740 --> 01:14:11.040
If you're shaping
shared experiences

01:14:11.040 --> 01:14:13.770
in art spaces for people
to participate in,

01:14:13.770 --> 01:14:16.860
this feels like a much more
personal or small scale

01:14:16.860 --> 01:14:17.520
experience.

01:14:17.520 --> 01:14:20.850
And for you, it also seems
very different from work

01:14:20.850 --> 01:14:21.870
that you've done before.

01:14:21.870 --> 01:14:24.120
So how would you
say that this is

01:14:24.120 --> 01:14:27.490
going to influence your
work going forward,

01:14:27.490 --> 01:14:29.640
and are the two kinds
of projects going

01:14:29.640 --> 01:14:30.870
to converge at some point?

01:14:30.870 --> 01:14:32.700
And do you think that
it's a path that you

01:14:32.700 --> 01:14:34.590
would like to continue along?

01:14:34.590 --> 01:14:37.260
There are several components
in the Little Sun Project

01:14:37.260 --> 01:14:40.170
that are different
from my other work,

01:14:40.170 --> 01:14:42.720
but there is also a lot
that is overlapping.

01:14:42.720 --> 01:14:48.870
The whole idea about questioning
or investigating self-esteem.

01:14:48.870 --> 01:14:52.710
The idea of feeling
resourceful, but also

01:14:52.710 --> 01:14:55.680
the idea of feeling
interconnected,

01:14:55.680 --> 01:15:00.100
the idea of sharing a project,
participating in a project.

01:15:00.100 --> 01:15:05.400
This lends itself very well to
online exchange of information.

01:15:05.400 --> 01:15:08.340
We have a lot of people who
through Facebook or some media

01:15:08.340 --> 01:15:11.070
uploads images that didn't
see that camera before.

01:15:11.070 --> 01:15:15.030
We had some, so there was
somebody just uploaded

01:15:15.030 --> 01:15:17.190
this picture, and
we're getting all that.

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:20.350
So looking at a
little closer, there's

01:15:20.350 --> 01:15:25.200
actually lots of components
which are in my other works.

01:15:27.850 --> 01:15:35.470
I feel that there will be an
increasing cross pollanization

01:15:35.470 --> 01:15:36.790
between the two.

01:15:36.790 --> 01:15:38.830
Also, I am having
my first one person

01:15:38.830 --> 01:15:42.933
show in the modern
museum of Addis Ababa.

01:15:42.933 --> 01:15:44.350
Had I not been
involved with this,

01:15:44.350 --> 01:15:45.767
I would not have
met these people.

01:15:48.820 --> 01:15:52.210
One of our great retailers
in Dakar, Senegal,

01:15:52.210 --> 01:15:54.100
she also has a gallery.

01:15:54.100 --> 01:15:58.700
So in an odd way, they're
not really very often,

01:15:58.700 --> 01:16:01.510
but sometimes there
is this situation.

01:16:01.510 --> 01:16:09.330
And yes, so for me, I don't
shift between the two.

01:16:09.330 --> 01:16:10.230
But it's interesting.

01:16:10.230 --> 01:16:13.170
And maybe also
something that I'm just

01:16:13.170 --> 01:16:17.010
trying to understand and
learn is that I suddenly

01:16:17.010 --> 01:16:19.170
have more contact
with politicians,

01:16:19.170 --> 01:16:23.250
the type of decisions makers
who are sort of in the policy

01:16:23.250 --> 01:16:27.990
world, the NGOs, the
UN, the kind of groups

01:16:27.990 --> 01:16:31.140
who are working with
awareness on energy related,

01:16:31.140 --> 01:16:31.950
health related.

01:16:31.950 --> 01:16:36.390
And it's very interesting.

01:16:36.390 --> 01:16:39.670
This year I went to Davos,
where we met, Nadia,

01:16:39.670 --> 01:16:42.060
and so in a very
interesting way,

01:16:42.060 --> 01:16:44.838
I can always look at
people, and I could say--

01:16:44.838 --> 01:16:46.380
so they asked me,
what are you doing?

01:16:46.380 --> 01:16:48.815
So a very Davos-ian
kind of question.

01:16:48.815 --> 01:16:49.440
What do you do?

01:16:49.440 --> 01:16:51.810
And I could say I'm
an artist, or I could

01:16:51.810 --> 01:16:55.320
say I'm in the energy sector.

01:16:55.320 --> 01:16:58.560
Or I could say I'm in
the bottom of pyramid

01:16:58.560 --> 01:17:02.198
economical sort of development,
or something like this.

01:17:02.198 --> 01:17:03.240
So it's very interesting.

01:17:03.240 --> 01:17:08.650
And I love this kind of
relativity of what you do,

01:17:08.650 --> 01:17:11.565
especially if what
one-- sometimes

01:17:11.565 --> 01:17:14.970
if you say I'm an artist,
people just go oh no.

01:17:14.970 --> 01:17:19.980
And you know, sometimes
it's also better

01:17:19.980 --> 01:17:22.230
not to say it's a work of
art because then people say,

01:17:22.230 --> 01:17:23.410
oh yeah, I see.

01:17:23.410 --> 01:17:24.810
It's a work of art.

01:17:24.810 --> 01:17:26.880
Nice.

01:17:26.880 --> 01:17:34.170
But yeah, I'm
learning a lot more

01:17:34.170 --> 01:17:39.480
than I used to only working in
the more conventional museums.

01:17:39.480 --> 01:17:41.110
That's for sure.

01:17:41.110 --> 01:17:41.610
All right.

01:17:41.610 --> 01:17:47.520
So I think on that important
note, we're at 6:30,

01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:50.700
and I know you have to
be at Media Labs by 7,

01:17:50.700 --> 01:17:55.360
and I want people to meet
you in person as well.

01:17:55.360 --> 01:17:58.570
So we will bring this
session to a close.

01:17:58.570 --> 01:18:01.590
And I will note that
one thing that's

01:18:01.590 --> 01:18:05.280
really important for all of
us who are working at the Data

01:18:05.280 --> 01:18:08.820
Center for Creating Solutions
for Resource Constrained

01:18:08.820 --> 01:18:15.120
Markets is to remember that
this is all, in some ways,

01:18:15.120 --> 01:18:16.200
about happiness.

01:18:16.200 --> 01:18:19.800
If we did it right, if
we met the human needs,

01:18:19.800 --> 01:18:23.910
I think a behavior
that we are engineering

01:18:23.910 --> 01:18:26.460
for is indeed happiness.

01:18:26.460 --> 01:18:29.710
And I hope we remember that
going out of this session.

01:18:29.710 --> 01:18:33.480
So thank you so much for
your generously spending

01:18:33.480 --> 01:18:34.680
so much time with us.

01:18:34.680 --> 01:18:35.950
No, thank you.

01:18:35.950 --> 01:18:37.740
Please come back,
and we would love

01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:39.360
to show you what
we've come up with,

01:18:39.360 --> 01:18:41.130
and get your thoughts on it.

01:18:41.130 --> 01:18:42.390
Thank you.

01:18:42.390 --> 01:18:46.040
[APPLAUSE]

